===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*skey-}}
From {{der|en|nl|scheen|t=strip of metal or wood; (''anatomy'') shin}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=4205484713|date=July 2023}}</ref> from {{der|en|dum|schene}}, from {{der|en|odt|*skina}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*skinu|t=bar; track; (''anatomy'') shin; spine}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*skinō|t=piece; plate; rim; (''anatomy'') shinbone}}, probably from {{der|en|ine-pro|*skey-|t=to dissect; to split}}. {{doublet|en|shin}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[thin#Adjective|thin]] [[strip#Noun|strip]] of an {{l|en|osier|t=[[long#Adjective|long]], [[pliable]] [[twig#Noun|twig]] from a [[plant#Noun|plant]], usually a [[willow#Noun|willow]]}} [[use#Verb|used]] in [[basketmaking]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1836|author=Luke Hebert|entry=BASKET|title=The Engineer’s and Mechanic’s Encyclopædia, Comprehending Practical Illustrations of the Machinery and Processes Employed in Every Description of Manufacture of the British Empire.{{nb...|With Nearly Two Thousand Engravings. [...] In Two Volumes.}}|location=London|publisher=Thomas Kelly,{{nb...|17, Paternoster Row.}}|volume=I|pages=153–154|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/engineersmechani01heberich/page/153/mode/1up|oclc=1064273888|passage=[F]or the finer work, as table-mats, fruit and work-baskets, and the like, the osiers are divided into four parts, lengthways, which are called splits, and these are afterwards reduced to various degrees of fineness, when they are called '''skeins'''.}}
# {{lb|en|US|road transport|dated}} A [[metallic#Adjective|metallic]] [[strengthen]]ing [[band#Noun|band]] or [[thimble#Noun|thimble]] on the [[wooden]] [[arm#Noun|arm]] of an [[axle]] of a [[wagon#Noun|wagon]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1862|author=w:Thomas Hughes|chapter=The Struggle for Kansas. The Substance of Two Lectures Delivered at the Working Men’s College, Great Ormond Street.|mainauthor=[[w:John Malcolm Forbes Ludlow|J[ohn] M[alcolm Forbes] Ludlow]]|title=A Sketch of the History of the United States from Independence to Secession.{{nb...|By J. M. Ludlow, [...] To which is Added, The Struggle for Kansas. By Thomas Hughes, [...]}}|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire; London|publisher=[[w:Macmillan Publishers|Macmillan and Co.]]|page=345|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sketchofhistoryo00ludliala/page/345/mode/1up|oclc=2567248|passage=On November 21, Dow, one of the free-state settlers went to the blacksmith's shop unarmed, carrying a waggon '''skein''' to be repaired. While he was at the shop, Coleman and Buckley of Missouri, and another pro-slavery man came up, all armed, and an angry discussion followed.}}

